{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-120831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-120831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})=0.411\\,516…≈0.411\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.411\\,5$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.411\\,5.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.411\\,5.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.411\\,5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/assets/ab3de51a23485b1776e014768b0775315694cd1147eb2772f71323d4d097dee2.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.411\\,5 \\\\  & ≈2.730\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈0.411\\,5\\cdot 2.730\\,1≈1.1,\n$$\n\nrounded to $1$ decimal place." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-120831" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ff46b8b5e043525577d8a79e8d68253196d427705e7bd342940712a2d8e6d76"} {"question" "q-120831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-120831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.1"] ["math" "1.2"] ["math" "1.3"] ["math" "2.1"] ["math" "3.1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-120831" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3})=-0.729\\,727…≈-0.7297\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-0.7297$ is not in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-0.7297.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-0.7297+2\\pi ≈5.5535\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/assets/4a49f40b94a408ea0094eb9f8fd065622e2aad91e56c2eba24071442d0d32e4c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈3.9,\\,x≈5.6.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ff46b8b5e043525577d8a79e8d68253196d427705e7bd342940712a2d8e6d76"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-331938" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5.6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies inside the domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/assets/1b1a3d9a84ba0d6da8d5c2c21a91a6027b1b57e520be7c886ecf5da1a9de1ee0.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are:\n\n$$\nx=-{150}^{∘},\\,x=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ff46b8b5e043525577d8a79e8d68253196d427705e7bd342940712a2d8e6d76"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-150"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-332377" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-30"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1=0,$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{3})=-0.339\\,836…≈-0.3398\n$$\n\nSince $-0.3398$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.3398.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.3398.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.3398.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/assets/ed9b300e3f4f79298feba6a2d61347d938ce64d7100ad7b0696e13dbe2a65238.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.3398 \\\\  & ≈3.4814\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-2\\pi  & ≈3.4814-2\\pi ≈-2.8018\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈-2.8,\\,x≈-0.3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ff46b8b5e043525577d8a79e8d68253196d427705e7bd342940712a2d8e6d76"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2.8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336322" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the exact solution to the equation  $2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$ Give your answer in degrees.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336511" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6ff46b8b5e043525577d8a79e8d68253196d427705e7bd342940712a2d8e6d76"} {"question" "q-336511" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"} {"question" "q-336511" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "90"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13970834/state.json" "file_sha256" "007fa9f025dae6a7227e136e174f5912976d1081147a52f62fbb12ead3e8401d"}] "usage" {} "basis" 659 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
